      "text": "To find the hypotenuse \\( h \\) of the right triangle and subsequently compute \\( 4^h \\), we will first convert the given logarithmic values to a common base. We will use the change of base formula:\n\n\\[\n\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate \\(\\log_4 27\\):\n\n\\[\n\\log_4 27 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 4} = \\frac{\\log_2 3^3}{2} = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(\\log_2 9\\):\n\n\\[\n\\log_2 9 = 2 \\log_2 3\n\\]\n\nNow, we use the Pythagorean theorem to find the hypotenuse \\( h \\):\n\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{(\\log_4 27)^2 + (\\log_2 9)^2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the expressions for \\((\\log_4 27)\\) and \\((\\log_2 9)\\):\n\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( 2 \\log_2 3 \\right)^2}\n\\]\n\nSimplify inside the square root:\n\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2 + (2 \\log_2 3)^2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2 + (2 \\log_2 3)^2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} + 2 \\log_2 3 \\right)^2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\left( 3 \\log_2 3 + 4 \\log_2 3 \\right)^2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{(7 \\log_2 3)^2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nh = 7 \\log_2 3\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\( 4^h \\):\n\n\\[\n4^h = 4^{7 \\log_2 3}\n\\]\n\nWe express 4 as a power of 2:\n\n\\[\n4 = 2^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 4^h = (2^2)^{7 \\log_2 3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n4^h = 2^{2 \\cdot 7 \\log_2 3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n4^h = 2^{14 \\log_2 3}\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms \\(a^{\\log_a b} = b\\):\n\n\\[\n2^{14 \\log_2 3} = (2^{\\log_2 3})^{14}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n2^{\\log_2 3} = 3\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\n\\[\n2^{14 \\log_2 3} = 3^{14}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3^{14}}\n\\]",
