    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The lengths of the legs of the triangle can be written as $\\log_4 27 = \\frac{3}{2} \\log_4 3$ and $\\log_2 9 = \\frac{2}{2} \\log_2 3.$\nBy the Pythagorean theorem, we have $h^2 = \\left(\\frac{3}{2} \\log_4 3\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{2}{2} \\log_2 3\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2 \\left(\\log_4 3\\right)^2 + 1 \\cdot \\log_2 3^2 = \\frac{9}{4} \\log_4 3^2 + 9 \\log_2 3.$\nSince $\\log_2 3^2 = 2 \\log_2 3,$ we have $h^2 = \\frac{9}{4} \\log_4 3^2 + 9 \\log_2 3 = \\frac{9}{4} (2 \\log_2^3) + 9 \\log_2 3 = 9 \\log_2 3 + \\frac{9}{2} + 9 \\log_2 3 = 18 \\log_2 3 + \\frac{9}{2}.$\nTo simplify, we factor out a 9 and multiply by 2 to get $h^2 = 36 \\log_2 3 + 9.$\nSince $\\log_4 3^2 = 2 \\log_4 3 = \\frac{1}{2} \\log_2 3,$ we have $h^2 = 36 \\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\log_2 3\\right) + 9 = 18 \\log_2 3 + 9.$\nExponentiating both sides of the equation $h = \\sqrt{18 \\log_2 3 + 9},$ we get $4^h = 4 \\cdot 4^{\\sqrt{18 \\log_2 3 + 9}} = 4 \\cdot \\left(4^{\\sqrt{18 \\log_2 3 + 9}}\\right).$\nSince $4^{\\sqrt{18 \\log_2 3 + 9}} = 4^{\\frac{9 + 2 \\sqrt{8}}{2}} = 4^{4+1} = 4^7 = 16384,$ we have $4^h = 4 \\cdot 16384 = \\boxed{65536}.$\nThe answer is: 65536",
